# 26. Research Priorities

The national baseline suggests the following priorities.

### Priority 1 — Construct the federal conservation funding inventory.

Identify major programs, agencies, funding authorities, and FY2020–FY2026 funding levels where comparable data exist.

### Priority 2 — Map dedicated revenue systems.

Especially:

* Pittman-Robertson;
* Dingell-Johnson;
* state hunting/fishing revenues;
* other dedicated conservation revenues.

### Priority 3 — Map private-land conservation funding.

Particularly Farm Bill programs and their geographic distribution.

### Priority 4 — Examine funding stability.

Determine which programs have:

* annual uncertainty;
* multi-year authority;
* dedicated revenue;
* temporary funding;
* maintenance gaps.

### Priority 5 — Examine geographic allocation.

Compare funding distribution with:

* ecological condition;
* species vulnerability;
* habitat;
* watershed impairment;
* restoration opportunity;
* conservation workload.

### Priority 6 — Examine administrative burden.

Identify whether smaller organizations face disproportionate barriers to accessing funding.

### Priority 7 — Connect funding to outcomes.

Select case studies where funding can be traced through implementation to ecological response.

